mple  theory okay again in the second part  what i want to do is sort of  operationalize to use social science  speak what i mean by hegemony what does  it mean to pursue hegemony in the modern  world  and the third thing that i want to do is  i want to shift to the subject of the  future of u.s chinese relations and talk  about what they're likely to look like  in the event that china continues to  econo to modernize economically in other  words it continues to grow  into a more powerful state economically  and i will argue militarily now it might  not seem at this point that all three of  those subjects are closely related but  as we go along here i think it'll become  manifestly apparent that that is the  case so back to the first subject which  is the question of why international  politics is a nasty and brutish business  where states seek hegemony my simple  theory  starts from what i think are five rather  non-controversial assumptions about the  structure of the international system  and what i argue is that when you take  those five simple assumptions you put  them into the blender and you hit the on  switch and you mix them up what you get  is a rather nasty world but by  themselves those five  assumptions  don't portray the world as particularly  competitive or com  or particularly brutish now what are the  five assumptions the first assumption  is that the international system  is dominated by states  and there is no higher authority that  sits above states yes there are  institutions like the united nations  uh there was once the league of nations  and these institutions do play some role  in international politics but they play  a minor role states are the principal  actors in the system and this system is  anarchic now when you use the word  anarchy people think you mean murder and  mayhem but as many of you know in  international relations that's not the  case to say that the system is simply  anarchic is to say that it has no higher  authority it's the opposite of  hierarchic so what we're saying is that  the international system is basically  comprised of states  that look like billiard bull balls on a  pool table right they have no higher  authority above them they are the  principal actors in the system that's  assumption number one  assumption number two  is that all states have some offensive  military capability now there's no  question that that military capability  varies from case to case but the fact is  that all states at all times have at  least some offensive military capability  so i like to say to people we usually  identify military capability with tanks  and aircraft carriers and guns but the  fact of the matter is that if you if  states were comprised of nothing more  than human beings  with loin cloths on as long as those  human beings had their feet and hands  they could walk across the border with  another state and use their hands to  kill people on the other side  for every nectar two hands to squeeze it  so the point is that states have some  offensive capability the third  assumption which is of great importance  is that states can never be certain  about the intentions of other states  in other words you can't be certain that  a state that  lives next door to you or lives  reasonably near to you at some point  down the road won't have malign  intentions  that's not to say you could be certain  that another state will have malign  intentions it's only to say that you  can't be certain that that state won't  have malign intentions for example  there's nobody in this audience who can  tell me what germany's intentions will  be in the year 2020 or what china's  intentions will be in the year 2020 or  even what america's intentions will be  in the year 2020. there's just no theory  no mechanism available to discern  intentions over the long run  to sort of emphasize this point from a  different perspective and it's a very  important point to grasp let me talk a  little bit about marriage and divorce  when people get married they almost  axiomatically assume that the person  they're marrying is wonderful  and that that person is going to be  wonderful forever  the fact that we have a 50 divorce rate  in this country tells you that that  assumption is not very sound the fact of  the matter is if you think about it  anytime you get married to someone it's  almost impossible to tell what he or she  is going to look like in terms of their  basic behavior towards you 20 or 30  years down the road the fact of the  matter is you may be marrying svengali  and just not know it and there is no way  you could figure out what your wife to  be or husband to be will look like in 20  or 30 years you can never be certain of  that person's intentions now that's not  to say you can be certain that you're  marrying a person who's going to turn on  you of course that's not the case my  only point is you can't be certain that  the person won't turn on you  first assumption is that the system is  anarchic it's comprised of states and  it's anarchic second assumption is that  states have some offensive military  capability and third is you can never be  certain about intentions fourth and  fifth assumptions are rather  straightforward fourth assumption is  that states are interested in survival  it's their highest goal this is not to  say states don't have other goals like  prosperity but their highest goal is  survival fifth assumption is that states  are basically strategic calculators  rational actors they are  pretty adept at coming up with clever  strategies for hit figuring out how to  survive in the international system  now those are the five assumptions and  as i said no one of those assumptions  that i've laid out says that the world  is highly competitive and that war is a  possible uh or a likely outcome  right  but when you take those five assumptions  and you put them into the blender and  you hit the on switch you get a very  competitive world to be more specific  you get three forms of behavior that  come right to the surface  first is that states fear each other  now why do states fear each other states  fear each other basically for two  reasons one they fear each other because  other states may have  hostile intentions to go along  with  their  offensive capabilities  and you can't be sure they won't come  after you in other words if you're  france and you live next door to germany  you can never be absolutely sure  right that the germany somewhere down  the road won't have hostile intentions  and build military forces and come after  you so that's one reason you're for fear  germany  the second reason that you fear germany  is the 911 problem  uh  this goes back to anarchy the fact of  the matter is that there's no higher  authority in the international system  that you can appeal to if you get into  trouble if you dialed 9-1-1 in  international politics you know who's at  the other end nobody  right  this leads to the second form of  behavior that results in this system  that i'm describing to you the first  being fear  second is that states quickly understand  that it's a self-help world god helps  those who help themselves in  international politics you can't rely on  other people to pull your chestnuts out  of the fire  because it just might not be in their  selfish interest when the money's on the  table for them to come to your rescue  and the end result is that you may end  up looking like france in 1870 or france  in 1940.  so the fact of the matter is that states  quickly decide that the best way to  protect themselves  is to protect themselves right and not  to rely on others  now the third form of behavior which is  the most important form of behavior that  comes out of this simple theory  is that the best way to ensure your  survival in the international system is  to be the most powerful state  in the international system to be the  hegemon to put it in more colloquial  terms the best way to survive in