ancer in other  regions and that's why people are  basically willing to tolerate us in  those regions because they know we're  not going to dominate those regions if  you talk to the europeans today the  europeans main concern is that nato is  fragmenting its fracturing the united  states is losing interest in europe it's  going to pull troops out of europe and  it's going to send them to fight the war  on terrorism or send them to fight a war  against asia they're not worried about  us dominating the european continent  they're worried about us going home it's  like the russians and the chinese  another excellent point that john raised  although i do think not surprisingly  that i have a response to john why  haven't the russians and the chinese  shown  much evidence that they're going to come  together against the united states  because we're not threatened to conquer  either one of them and furthermore  because they basically share a border  and have all sorts of problems between  themselves they understand that they're  more likely to go to war with each other  than they are to go to war with the  united states the offshore balancer so  that's the argument i would make as to  why there's been no balancing against us  thanks john uh  last question to professor walter rice  from george washington university thank  you it's a  well let me say that i'm  i'm delighted that i haven't yet read  your book john because i have the  pleasure  of looking forward to it  but i just want to understand how  radical a structuralist you are  you describe a situation  in which things are inevitable  sort of like when you catch a cold  your immune system is unless it's sick  is going to attack whatever is causing  your cold  does it make does it not make a  difference who runs a particular state  does it not make are they are states  always going to behave  uh in a certain way because of the  structural system as if they've already  read your book  are things so inevitable  that's 