s behave in  rather heavy-handed and cold and  calculating ways towards each other and  i think by the way this is especially  true of the united states but what the  united states has is it has this whole  stable of spin doctors who come out and  justify all of our cold and calculating  behavior in very idealistic rhetoric  which the american people just gobble up  because we all want to believe that  we're the good guys and everybody else  is the bad guys you go outside the  borders of the united states you go to  asia you go to europe and you talk to  people about what they think about  american mock politique they think the  united states is a very heavy-handed and  in many ways a very nasty country in  terms of its foreign policy behavior i'm  not condemning that by the way my point  is that that's the way you have to  behave in the international system to  survive same is true with a country like  israel israel is a really tough nut  these people you know  they really uh  act according to the dictates of  offensive realism and they do it because  they're interested in surviving in a  nasty neighborhood same thing is true  with india despite all this talk about  mahatma gandhi and  peaceful  uh protests and so forth and so on the  indians have consistently behaved like  the israelis like the americans like the  british in a very heavy-handed kind of  way very much according to the dictates  of realism but our great advantage in  this world the great advantage all four  of those countries have is that they  have terrific spin doctors  well this john this has been a terrific  exchange the book is the tragedy of  great power politics by john professor  john mearsheim of the university of  chicago available through norton press  thank you john mearsheimer thank you  john ikebere for an outstanding  commentary and please join me in  thanking our speakers today for a  terrific session  now i would invite you all  i invite you all to join us  for a reception just acro