mple  theory okay again in the second part  what i want to do is sort of  operationalize to use social science  speak what i mean by hegemony what does  it mean to pursue hegemony in the modern  world  and the third thing that i want to do is  i want to shift to the subject of the  future of u.s chinese relations and talk  about what they're likely to look like  in the event that china continues to  econo to modernize economically in other  words it continues to grow  into a more powerful state economically  and i will argue militarily now it might  not seem at this point that all three of  those subjects are closely related but  as we go along here i think it'll become  manifestly apparent that that is the  case so back to the first subject which  is the question of why international  politics is a nasty and brutish business  where states seek hegemony my simple  theory  starts from what i think are five rather  non-controversial assumptions about the  structure of the international system  and what i argue is that when you take  those five simple assumptions you put  them into the blender and you hit the on  switch and you mix them up what you get  is a rather nasty world but by  themselves those five  assumptions  don't portray the world as particularly  competitive or com  or particularly brutish now what are the  five assumptions the first assumption  is that the international system  is dominated by states  and there is no higher authority that  sits above states yes there are  institutions like the united nations  uh there was once the league of nations  and these institutions do play some role  in international politics but they play  a minor role states are the principal  actors in the system and this system is  anarchic now when you use the word  anarchy people think you mean murder and  mayhem but as many of you know in  international relations that's not the  case to say that the system is simply  anarchic is to say that it has no higher  authority it's 