nd divorce  when people get married they almost  axiomatically assume that the person  they're marrying is wonderful  and that that person is going to be  wonderful forever  the fact that we have a 50 divorce rate  in this country tells you that that  assumption is not very sound the fact of  the matter is if you think about it  anytime you get married to someone it's  almost impossible to tell what he or she  is going to look like in terms of their  basic behavior towards you 20 or 30  years down the road the fact of the  matter is you may be marrying svengali  and just not know it and there is no way  you could figure out what your wife to  be or husband to be will look like in 20  or 30 years you can never be certain of  that person's intentions now that's not  to say you can be certain that you're  marrying a person who's going to turn on  you of course that's not the case my  only point is you can't be certain that  the person won't turn on you  first assumption is that the system is  anarchic it's comprised of states and  it's anarchic second assumption is that  states have some offensive military  capability and third is you can never be  certain about intentions fourth and  fifth assumptions are rather  straightforward fourth assumption is  that states are interested in survival  it's their highest goal this is not to  say states don't have other goals like  prosperity but their highest goal is  survival fifth assumption is that states  are basically strategic calculators  rational actors they are  pretty adept at coming up with clever  strategies for hit figuring out how to  survive in the international system  now those are the five assumptions and  as i said no one of those assumptions  that i've laid out says that the world  is highly competitive and that war is a  possible uh or a likely outcome  right  but when you take those five assumptions  and you put them into the blender and  you hit the on switch you get a very  competitive world to be more specific  you get three forms of behavior that  come right to the surface  first is that states fear each other  now why do states fear each other states  fear each other basically for two  reasons one they fear each other because  other states may have  hostile intentions to go along  with  their  offensive capabilities  and you can't be sure they won't come  after you in other words if you're  france and you live next door to germany  you can never be absolutely sure  right that the germany somewhere down  the road won't have hostile intentions  and build military forces and come after  you so that's one reason you're for fear  germany  the second reason that you fear germany  is the 911 problem  uh  this goes back to anarchy the fact of  the matter is that there's no higher  authority in the international system  that you can appeal to if you get into  trouble if you dialed 9-1-1 in  international politics you know who's at  the other end nobody  right  this leads to the second form of  behavior that results in this system  that i'm describing to you the first  being fear  second is that states quickly understand  that it's a self-help world god helps  those who help themselves in  international politics you can't rely on  other people to pull your chestnuts out  of the fire  because it just might not be in their  selfish interest when the money's on the  table for them to come to your rescue  and the end result is that you may end  up looking like france in 1870 or france  in 1940.  so the fact of the matter is that states  quickly decide that the best way to  protect themselves  is to protect themselves right and not  to rely on others  now the third form of behavior which is  the most important form of behavior that  comes out of this simple theory  is that the best way to ensure your  survival in the international system is  to be the most powerful state  in the international system to be the  hegemon to put it in more colloquial  terms the best way to survive in the  international system is to be the  biggest and baddest dude on the block  i'll get to this in a second but to get  ahead of myself  best situation you could possibly have  is to be the united states in the  western hemisphere how many of you  people go to bed at night worrying about  canada or mexico attacking us how many  of you worry about cuba or guatemala or  brazil or argentina attacking us the  answer is nobody worries about that why  because we are so powerful that none of  those states would ever think about  attacking us would you substitute a  situation where we had china on our  border and germany on our other border  of course not we liked the situation we  now have in the western hemisphere  perfectly well because we are by far the  most powerful state in the region  the basic line that i'm trying to get  across here is that the more powerful  you are  relative  to all of your potential adversaries the  more secure you are because it's highly  unlikely that they will come after you  now the fact of the matter is that every  great power in the system understands  the basic logic of this situation and  every great power strives to be the  biggest and baddest dude on the block  the end result of all this is that you  get security competition sometimes very  intense security competition among the  great powers  not in large part because great powers  are ativistic at heart or have malign  intentions but because in the  international system where you have a  significant 9-1-1 problem the best way  to assure your survival is to be the  hegemon in the system  but again everybody gets this message so  you get this constant competition for  power this constant competition among  the players to see who is going to be  the hegemon and be in the best position  to assure  survival  that's the basic theory  now i want to talk  more about the subject of hegemony and  this is the second part of my talk i've  made much to you about the fact  that states want to be