m  deterrence  and balanced allocation of resources to  the military establishment vis-a-vis the  rest of the  economy  so that's the first sort of set of  difficulties i have secondly  i don't think it quite understands  chinese history um  the philosophical basis of chinese  statesmanship and particularly the role  that hegemony plays in chinese thinking  you will find the term ba  in chinese vocabulary before you will  find the word hedge in greek  it goes back to the 4th century bc it is  something the chinese have discussed  ever since  and it is fundamental to their thinking  about international affairs and it is  the cinequan non of contemporary chinese  foreign policy that is to say  fanba  anti-hegemony  and they are against it in a generic  sense not just because the united states  and therefore practices it  this is rooted very much in the century  and a half of their so-called shame and  humiliation this is a country that has a  deep insular psychology  that has been subjected to hegemony over  a very long period of time  and has a it certainly is a is a  nationalist country we know about that  but i would submit to you it's what i  call defensive nationalism rather than  aggressive nationalism  um thirdly i don't think you quite uh  defined very well chinese dominance of  asia or hegemony over asia as you  predict it  i find the argument much to zero sum  what about japan what about india what  about russia and who why should you  assume the united states is going to  pack up and go home  just because the chinese want to assert  their hegemony over the region  and last point i would make is the u.s  policy towards china the project i mean  i will grant you very much it's that  it's not just the republican business  community  and the clinton  liberals uh who have had a missionary i  call it the missionary impulse to  transform china and america's image this  goes back to the 1860s  we've had a long-standing  attempt in this country to try and do  that it's not about to end anytime soon  but i would submit to you for the sake  of this discussion that it's a strategy  of co-optation not preemption and not  containment and from nixon through the  six subsequent administrations and we  have a president going to china next  week  who now articulates the same line of  arguments about integration of china  into the international system and  engagement of china  to try and build interdependencies  between the united states and china  this is not a strategy of preemption or  prevention or much less containment so  those are my observations i don't think  it holds water  when it comes to china my question for  you is why can't the co-optation  strategy if you want to call it that  work  why would the united states abdicate  east asia to china and why don't the  interdependencies between the united  states and china which are increasingly  deep 120 billion dollars in annual trade  just to name one  why won't that why won't that ensure a  less zero-sum future  the problem with those questions is that  there are so many of them uh they have a  whole session just on that yeah i could  have a whole session so i'm gonna go  very quickly yeah first of all i was  assuming that china will grow  economically into a giant hong kong  you're correct to point out that that  might not happen for economic reasons  and maybe even for political reasons  maybe the country will fragment and  break apart  i was just assuming that it did continue  to grow economically and then ask what  would be the consequences of that  from my point of view i hope it doesn't  grow economically and i hope it doesn't  turn into a jug or not  it was just an assumption  with regard to  the history of china  first of all i don't really care that  much to be honest about the history of  china because i'm a structuralist right  and i believe that the structure of the  situation dictates how you behave  and  my argument is that yet china has not  been very aggressive 