kly  cobble together a balancing coalition to  prevent that from happening now you  believe like many china specialists that  this would be a terrible thing because  we should really engage them  and hopefully we'll all live happily  ever after there's one fundamental  problem with your argument and that is  that you have no way of being able to  tell me what chinese intentions will  look like in the year 2020 or in the  year 2030. so from an american  perspective you're talking about  creating let's put it slightly  differently helping to create a  juggernaut  with which you can't tell me how it will  behave  this to me is not a smart policy i do  not create juggernauts unless i am  absolutely certain that they won't come  after me and no chinese specialist  despite all his or her knowledge about  the subject has ever come up with a way  of telling me what chinese intentions  will look like in the year 2020. given  that fact i say let's contain them now  i want to  break in a few minutes to go to the  reception where we can continue this uh  dialogue but i'm going to take two more  questions of bob lieber from georgetown  university first  in  walters um a two-part question and also  let me add my voice to those who  having read the book see it as a real  masterpiece  my questions though are are twofold  one  about the revolution in military affairs  an important part of your book which you  did not touch on at length in the talk  it has to do with the stopping power of  water which you then relate to the  notion that becoming a global hegemon is  a very very difficult if not impossible  my question is in light of the rma  whether that assumption needs to be  re-examined and somewhat connected to it  is to ask your your thoughts about the  post september 11th world the last  portion of your talk as the last portion  of your book focused on the subject of  the united states and china over the  long term  but in view of the dramatic and  even traumatic events of september 11th  and the  major  perhaps reconceptualizing of american  grand strategy by the bush  administration i'd be curious to hear  your thoughts about that subject  uh thank you for your kind comments bob  about the book and for other people as  well especially john for all his kind  words  uh first on the revolution in military  affairs  i have never been too sure exactly what  the revolution in military affairs is  number one and number two i'm never too  sure whether it's happened  is happening or is about to happen  but uh having said all those things i  kind of guess it means that all these  precision guided munitions  uh that we use  uh in places like yugoslavia  in 1999 in afghanistan have  revolutionized warfare and have given us  tremendous power  that we heretofore did not have  now  i think that precision guided munitions  really do matter  and i don't want to say that they're  insignificant  but i think that taking the united  states air force and going up against  yugoslavia or the taliban  and you know beating the stuffing out of  them doesn't really tell you too much  about how powerful you are  you know this is like the vermont  against the apaches it's just not really  a fair fight  and i wouldn't read too much into what  happened in afghanistan i would also  note that the pentagon and the bush  administration in general have just not  given us a lot of information about how  that war played itself out  and i'm one of these people who likes  lots of facts and likes to see the whole  story before i make up my mind on  exactly how important particular weapons  are or particular military strategies or  doctrines are in winning wars  and  i wouldn't be surprised if when all said  and done that we conclude that air power  really did matter greatly in afghanistan  but not as greatly as we thought in  november and december and maybe even  today i mean i may be wrong on that but  i want to see more information but again  my bottom line is that if you're 