 sort of why  didn't  the world balance the united states at  the end of the cold war in the way that  realist theory uh would uh predict  yeah john raised a number of uh  excellent points and being the smart guy  that he is he sort of zeroed in on what  are all the  potentially weak points in my argument  and i wish i had uh at least a half hour  to deal with them one by one  let me just deal with two points  picking up on rob  first of all i think that the reason  that europe looks so peaceful today and  in fact asia looks so peaceful today is  you have a hundred thousand american  troops sitting on top of europe and a  hundred thousand troops sitting on top  of asia and in fact those hundred  thousand troops in europe are sitting on  top of germany which in terms of latent  power is the potentially most powerful  state in europe in japan which is  potentially the most powerful state in  asia at this point in time the united  states sits on top of them as well  nobody has any elbow room everybody is  basically in a straitjacket except the  russians and the chinese who are in no  position to contest uncle sam as long as  he remains firmly in place in europe and  asia so i don't think you have a lot of  security competition in either one of  those regions in large part because the  american pacifier is there this leads to  rob's question which of course is the  point up point that john raised which is  why hasn't anybody balanced against us  nobody balances against us because we're  not out to be a global hegemon right we  are an offshore balancer  right  there's much talk and  uh john i mentioned the paul kennedy  piece in the financial times which i  read which is an excellent piece but you  read that piece and you read a lot of  the uh papers stories in the papers  these days and listen to people talk  there's this sort of tendency to  describe us as a global hegemon  we're not a global hegemon we're a  hegemon in the western hemisphere and  we're an offshore balancer in other  regions and that's why people are  basically willing to tolerate us in  those regions because they know we're  not going to dominate those regions if  you talk to the europeans today the  europeans main concern is that nato is  fragmenting its fracturing the united  states is losing interest in europe it's  going to pull troops out of europe and  it's going to send them to fight the war  on terrorism or send them to fight a war  against asia they're not worried about  us dominating the european continent  they're worried about us going home it's  like the russians and the chinese  another excellent point that john raised  although i do think not surprisingly  that i have a response to john why  haven't the russians and the chinese  shown  much evidence that they're going to come  together against the united states  because we're not threatened to conquer  either one of them and furthermore  because they basically share a border  and have all sorts of problems between  themselves they understand that they're  more likely to go to war with each other  than they are to go to war with the  united states the offshore balancer so  that's the argument i would make as to  why there's been no balancing against us  thanks john uh  last question to professor walter rice  from george washington university thank  you it's a  well let me say that i'm  i'm delighted that i haven't yet read  your book john because i have the  pleasure  of looking forward to it  but i just want to understand how  radical a structuralist you are  you describe a situation  in which things are inevitable  sort of like when you catch a cold  your immune system is unless it's sick  is going to attack whatever is causing  your cold  does it make does it not make a  difference who runs a particular state  does it not make are they are states  always going to behave  uh in a certain way because of the  structural system as if they've already  read your book  are things so inevitable  that's 